AB  - To ensure the safety of hydrogen energy systems, real-time, non-destructive failure detection in high-pressure storage tanks is essential. This study proposes a diagnostic framework for Type III hydrogen tanks, composed of a metallic liner and a Carbon Fiber Reinforced Polymer(CFRP) shell. Unlike conventional CFRP failure analyses based on peak frequency labeling of Acoustic Emission(AE) signals—an approach with limitations—we constructed a high-fidelity failure dataset by applying representation learning to AE signals obtained under tensile loading. Conventional diagnostics for Type III tanks have relied on simulation-based methods like Finite Element Modeling(FEM), which are computationally expensive and unsuitable for real-time detection. Our method addresses five major failure modes: elasticity, plasticity, fiber breakage, matrix cracks, and delamination. For CFRP failure labeling, we applied deep learning-based representation learning and achieved reasonable average clustering performance (average silhouette coefficient = 0.525, average Davies-Bouldin index = 0.678). A multi-modal classifier combining waveform and frequency features was developed using a 1-Dimensional Convolutional Neural Network(1D-CNN) to exploit their complementarity. The model achieved 96.71 % accuracy and an harmonic mean of precision and recall(F1-score) of 0.9627. Class imbalance was addressed with the Synthetic Minority Over-sampling Technique(SMOTE), and inference completed in 48.49 milliseconds (ms) per waveform, enabling real-time detection. All experiments were conducted in a controlled laboratory environment; future work will explore validation in operational hydrogen tanks. This framework enables scalable, accurate failure detection, contributing to enhanced safety and reliability in hydrogen storage infrastructure.
