AB  - Although large language models have shown noticeable capability in open areas like translation and text classification, psychological theories are still required, therefore the threshold for emotional computing is high. This research focuses on children’s difficult problem of emotion perception by developing a child-specific model to improve the performance on visual question responses and facial expression categorization. We present a new visual-language model trained with text describing children’s traits and visual aspects, based on the bootstrapping language-image pretraining architecture. The child psychologist’s written instructions serve as a guide for the training process. To increase accuracy and achieve lower memory and calculation costs, the model is further fine-tuned and refined using the low-rank adaptation method. Three public children’s emotion classification and seven child psychology survey datasets are used to create the instruction and test the model performance. The outcomes demonstrate our models’ clear superiority over traditional deep learning models across all datasets. Following training, our multi-modal models extract meaningful visual information and demonstrate picture understanding, which is more sophisticated than categorization, demonstrating a knowledge of emotional perception.
