KW  - Cooperative co-evolutionary algorithm
AB  - The energy-aware scheduling problem has garnered growing interest among researchers in the manufacturing domain. However, there is a notable scarcity of studies that specifically address the energy-aware scheduling problem within the context of a hybrid assembly job shop environment. In such a workshop, sequential operation precedence constraints and hierarchical job precedence constraints are considered in an integrated manner, distinguishing it from conventional scheduling problems and intensifying the complexity of the issue. Additionally, considering strong interdependence between production and logistics tasks in practical manufacturing, the limited utilization of automated guided vehicles (AGVs) is also taken into consideration. Therefore, this paper focuses on an energy-aware hybrid assembly job shop scheduling problem with a limited number of automated guided vehicles (EA-HAJSP-AGV). A novel mathematical model is proposed to minimize the comprehensive energy consumption in the workshop. Subsequently, a type of cooperative co-evolutionary algorithm (CCEA) with different collaborator selection methods is developed. Some problem-oriented search operators are also designed considering the specific characteristics of the problem. Extensive experiments are conducted to evaluate the performance of various collaborator selection methods. Then comprehensive computational results and statistical analyses consistently demonstrate the superiority of our CCEA compared to other algorithms.
